# Authorization

Source of truth: `docs/architecture/authorization.md`.

## Model

- **Permissions** are the check unit: `tenant.members.invite`, `authorization.roles.manage`
- **Roles** are tenant-scoped permission bundles on a membership
- **Policies** cover resource/attribute rules (“may edit *this* membership”)

## Where Checks Live

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| API | Coarse `@RequirePermission('…')` — fast reject |
| Application | Authoritative check via `AuthorizationPort` (+ resource policies) |
| Domain | Business invariants only (not permission strings) |
| Frontend | `useCan` / `<Can>` — **UX only** |

```typescript
// API
@RequirePermission('tenant.members.invite')
@Post()
invite(@Body() body: InviteMemberInputDto) { /* … */ }

// Application
await this.authz.require(cmd.actorUserId, 'tenant.members.invite', {
  tenantId: cmd.tenantId,
})
```

## Frontend

Backend `/me` returns **effective permissions** for the active tenant. FE does not re-derive from roles.

```typescript
const canInvite = useCan('tenant.members.invite')
return <Button disabled={!canInvite}>Invite</Button>
```

## Forbidden

```typescript
// ❌ Check role names in business logic
if (user.role === 'Admin') { … }

// ❌ FE-only security (hide button, skip backend check)
// ❌ Permission checks only in controllers (workers bypass them)
```
